-- title:
--   Killing an idempotent costs one variable and one relation
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $N$ be a natural number, and let $f \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,N \to R[x_0,\dots,x_{N-1}]$ be a family of $N$ polynomials in $N$ variables; write $Q = R[x_0,\dots,x_{N-1}]/I$ where $I$ is the ideal generated by the set of values of $f$. Let $e \in Q$ be an element with $e \cdot e = e$. The assertion is that there exists a family $f' \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,(N+1) \to R[x_0,\dots,x_N]$ of $N+1$ polynomials in $N+1$ variables such that the type of $R$-algebra isomorphisms from $R[x_0,\dots,x_N]/J$, with $J$ the ideal generated by the set of values of $f'$, to the quotient $Q/(e)$ of $Q$ by the ideal generated by the single element $e$ is nonempty; that is, the two $R$-algebras are isomorphic, with no particular isomorphism exhibited in the statement. The number of generators of $J$ thus again equals the number of variables.
--
--   This records that a "square" presentation — as many relations as variables — of a commutative $R$-algebra survives passage to the quotient by an idempotent, the point being that $Q/(e)$ is the localisation of $Q$ at the complementary idempotent $1-e$ and so needs only one extra variable and one extra relation. It is used by [`PDivisibleGroup.exists_square_presentation_level_of_ringOfIntegers`](thm.html#PDivisibleGroup.exists_square_presentation_level_of_ringOfIntegers) to produce square presentations at finite level.
